     3	# its own recorder on a network the agent cannot see.
     4	#
     5	# Read this as a list of the claims the design makes, each turned into a command that either
     6	# succeeds or does not. It is deliberately shallow: it does not try to prove an agent cannot
     7	# escape, it checks that the walls are where the documentation says they are.
     8	#
     9	#   sh scripts/verify_containment.sh                    # every running agent_*
    10	#   AGENTS="agent_1 agent_4" sh scripts/...             # chosen agents
    11	#   COMPOSE="docker compose -p <project> --env-file <file> -f <a> -f <b>" sh scripts/...
    12	#
    13	# COMPOSE is the whole compose command prefix, so the script checks whichever project it names (the
    14	# smoke stack in live/stack.py passes its own). It is split on spaces, so no path in it may
    15	# contain one. `--all` is accepted and ignored: every running agent is the default now.
    16	#
    17	# The real key is the one secret here. It never enters an agent's container -- the agent is what is
    18	# contained, and a key piped into a process there is a key handed to a same-uid reader of that
    19	# process -- and it is never held in a shell variable, passed as an argument or printed. The agent's
    20	# files come out as a tar stream, and a host-side search reads the key from `docker inspect` itself.
    21	#
    22	# Every check fails closed. A negative probe ("cannot connect", "cannot unlink") prints a verdict
    23	# word from inside the container, and only that word passes: an exec that did not run, or ran and
    24	# said nothing, is a FAIL, never evidence that the wall holds.
